# ─── Merkle claim artifacts — generated, NEVER committed ─────────────────────
# `apps/web/public/` is served statically, so a committed proofs.json would be
# published at /claim-data/proofs.json and read as the AUTHORITATIVE claim
# table. Shipping a fixture or dry-run build as the real allocation is the exact
# failure this rule exists to prevent. Publishing the real table must be a
# conscious `git add -f`, never a side effect of `git add -A` — see
# claim-data/RUNBOOK.md step 1.
#
# The two halves are deliberately asymmetric, because the two directories are:
#
#   - `apps/web/public/claim-data/` is blanket-ignored. Nothing under it is ever
#     meant to be tracked, and it is web-served, so a wildcard is the safer
#     shape: it also catches any NEW file a future generator writes there.
#
#   - the root `claim-data/` is enumerated instead, because it holds three files
#     that ARE deliberately tracked — RUNBOOK.md, DRYRUN.md and the dry-run
#     receipts under dryrun/. A blanket `/claim-data/` would ignore a directory
#     this repo tracks files in, and would silently hide later runbook edits.
#     The list below is exact, not a guess: `scripts/build-merkle.ts` writes
#     tree.json, root.txt, summary.txt (lines 338-340) and public/proofs.json
#     (line 355) — those four paths and nothing else.
claim-data/tree.json
claim-data/root.txt
claim-data/summary.txt
apps/web/public/claim-data/

# The throwaway deployer key for the testnet dry run. Already covered by the
# `.env.*` rule above, and repeated here on purpose: that rule matches this
# file by coincidence of naming, and a private key should not be protected by
# a coincidence. The dry-run RECEIPTS (claim-data/dryrun/*.json) are NOT
# ignored — they are the evidence the dry run happened and are meant to ship.
claim-data/dryrun/.env.deployer
